re highly  political and  explicitly so actually and so you had  this feeling  very very difficult feeling for a great  heritage organization that the people in  charge of it actually don't like  their properties and that's very  demoralizing  and uh it makes people feel that the  whole purpose of the  trust which has sustained it for more  than 100 years is being um  undermined what's incredible is that you  mentioned in your piece that  on the panel of people who put together  this report there were no academic  historians and indeed  some of the leading academia surrounding  this very complicated and nuanced  history  weren't cited at all so i mean who did  who did do the report in the end  well the report was mostly written by  national trust  curators and i think that was a  deliberate decision by the trust to show  that their people were endorsing it  and those curators are often very good  at their particular subjects but there's  no special reason why the expert on  textiles  should be able to talk about the  politics of imperial history  um or furniture or whatever it may be  and the  guiding influence behind the report is  who  is professor corin fowler of the  university of leicester  who's not a historian um she's a  professor of post-colonial literature  not on the staff of the trust but  retained by the trust  to help with these matters and she has a  very very clear very hard-line  which she expresses in her book with the  revealing title of green unpleasant land  um in wishing to show that the patrimony  of  britain and particularly england is  a slave um slavery polluted  empire polluted patrimony and therefore  sort of wishes to cast a shadow  over the many beautiful places and  buildings  um and the whole history really which  the national trust  cherishes um i think it's entirely  appropriate  that a body like the national trust  should understand these things and not  try to hide anything  but i think it's quite inappropriate  that it should give a platform  to people who are actually against  the very things it tries to protect  conserve and celebrate  and it even does this in its educational  program so professor fowler  um has this program which the trust is a  national trust program  called the colonial countryside project  in which they take school children 10  year olds  round houses that they say are linked  with slavery  or what they call colonialism and invite  the children to have a look at it and to  write creative writing which of course  because the children sort of know what's  expected of them is  very very hostile um to the places and  sometimes to the  um occupants the former occupants of the  places who often actually gave their  properties  to the trust so you have a poem saying  what a  dreadful man lord kirsten was who's one  of the greatest benefactors of the trust  ever  and of conservation in general both in  india and in britain  and so you have this feeling of  hostility right within the system  to what the national trust stands for  and it's not  necessarily um therefore because it's  motivated by that hostility it's not  necessarily learned it's not necessarily  well informed or accurate  lots of things they said were about  winston churchill for example and  chartwell were wrong factually wrong  and um this also is very undermining  uh it's simply not correct um and  of course the millions and millions of  members the membership now declining  uh will worry about that rightly so  so if you were a historian with a  different perspective  you mentioned this campaign group  restore trust  were you consulted because it is a very  nuanced  view on a very complicated and long  history and  i can imagine a lot of these questions  are quite hard to answer you know how do  you say if an economy is  quote unquote based on slavery or  colonialism  when you know everything gets muddled  together quite a bit  did they consult a wide range of  opinions  no i wouldn't i wouldn't say they did  because everybody in the colonial  countryside project  and pretty much everybody in the interim  report  are of only one view um and they  as far as i can see made no effort to  get  wider views i think the way to approach  this subject is  to think more about the whole historical  context of the acquisition of all sorts  of property  by the national trust and why it  mattered what it tells you where it  comes from and so on  and then you will find that  there are all sorts and some of them are  just as controversial as slavery believe  it or not i mean the classic one which  could easily be addressed by the  national trust is the dissolution of the  monasteries in which  um by killing and theft really state  theft  monastic institutions were destroyed the  monks were sometimes hanged  and their property was grabbed by rich  friends who then became richer  of henry viii um now that  could be presented as a tremendous stain  on some of the trust properties such as  a phantom's abbey for example  um but i think a sensible historical  approach to that would not be concealed  it should be told but not to try to work  up a sort of lazar of indignation  about the whole thing the story of  country houses the story of the  countryside is often to do with power  and money and sometimes it's to do with  robbery and violence and that's  important to understand but give it the  full context don't single out  um a couple of things that happen to  obsess you  and also don't link one of them slavery  and empire as  almost identical concepts they're jolly  well not and a great many people who  serve the empire  did very great things in the world i  can't think of anything great about  slavery but i can think of  many great things in the world done by  the british empire and by  imperial servants and one of those great  things by the way was the abolition  of slavery so let's have some nuance  let's have some context let's have some  depth and breadth  and let's also remember that the trust  is about objects and places  it needs to give them their wider  historical context but it is not  a political body it's not about the  history of parliament the history of war  the history of  of um industry all these things  come into it it is about the these  objects these places  um these beautiful landscapes these  beautiful houses and works of art  and that's what it's good at and that's  what it should  maintain with love do you think that  there is more that the national trust  could do when it comes to diversity i  mean the plight of catholic monks  in the middle ages is probably not as  fashionable these days as  multiculturalism and people from various  communities i'm always amused when i go  to the national trust that  my family are the only non-white people  visiting do you so do you think there is  more that the trust could do to  quote unquote use that awful phrase  modernize  well i think that um first of all i  don't think it's surprising because on  the whole  ethnic minority populations still live  more in towns  and are less likely to be close to a  great many uh national trust  properties but i think there's something  perverse going on here  if you start to preach that these houses  are based on iniquity  why would that persuade um  ethnic minorities who are supposed to be  the butt of this iniquity  to come yes very true if you you know if  you  um if you're proud of your heritage  though also honest about it  that's what attracts people of of any  race you know i don't have a problem if  i go to  um if i'm an ethnic minority in india or  whatever when i visit  i want to see the great um  uh examples of indian culture and i  don't have to keep thinking to myself  am i quote uncomfortable that's one of  the words these people love  uncomfortable  here no um i am i'm interested  and but of course i'm not going to be  interested if i'm always um  told how evil it is yes absolutely and  finally because i know we have to let  you go very very  soon there's now a national trust  chairmanship  empty would you throw would you fancy  throwing your hat in the ring  no no i mean um uh i think there will  now be a long process  actually there should be a long process  where they need to think very carefully  and they need to find someone who um  really can recognize how badly things  have gone wrong  and put them right but not i hope in a  terribly confrontational way it's got to  be firm  but it's also got to be healing because  the great tremendous  historical advantage of the national  trust is in its name it's national and  it's trusted  and that trust has been greatly  diminished  in some cases positively broken and  the next people have to really work hard  to restore it  charles moore thanks for coming on to  spectator tv and finally  would you eat meat grown in a lab last  december a world first was created when  a singaporean restaurant  sold a box of chicken nuggets that were  completely lab-grown  so-called cultured meat is the future  and means that we won't have to kill  animals for food in the years to come  at least that's what the mp anthony  brown argues in his article for the  spectator this week  i'm joined now by dr mark cotter from  cambridge and also co-founder  of meetable a company which is on track  to produce cultured pork  in just a couple of years dr cotter  welcome to spectator tv  can you start by explaining to the  layman what cultured meat  means hi cindy thank you very much for  having me on the show  so cultured meat is really the concept  of  creating real meat  in a lab um removing the requirement  of large-scale and animal farming  can you explain what the life process of  that looks like how how do you grow like  meeting a lab so i think we've seen sort  of  meat substitutes become popular in the  past couple of years and these are often  based on plant-based sources  um cultured meat really wants to create  the real thing  the real thing which is consists mainly  of muscle  tissue and and fat tissue  um and remove the sort of requirement of  animal farming do this actually in in a  lab  and the way that this works is that you  use  bioreactors which are you know culture  conditions  uh um that um have the cells floating  in 3d and and allowing them  to grow up and then um you want to  put them into molds essentially  that allow them to grow muscle bundles  and fat tissue i think that would be the  ideal way of doing this  sounds very simple it's actually very  very difficult um  and and there's been sort of slow  pro progress until more recently when  when things really sped up  well that brings me to my next question  actually which is how far away  is this technology i think we made some  leaps uh um recently and  i i can i can tell you about uh the  company that i'm involved in  which is called mutable essentially it  came out  of an invention it was created based on  an invention that  my academic laboratory did together with  professor ludovic's laboratory at the  stem cell institutes in cambridge  um and what this allowed us to do is to  really make this process of creating  these fat cells and these and these  and these muscle cells very very  efficiently that's been really  the bottleneck so being able to create  um specific cell types is not only a  problem  for cultured meat it's also a problem in  other fields like medicine  you know i'm my bet my background is a  medical doctor  so that's that was my primary interest  but we happen to discover a process  that is very very efficient and scalable  and so the idea of meetable is to  to develop this process to create these  uh products  um meat products at scale and how far  are we away  i believe we're going to see first  prototypes  on display um this year and i think  there'll be there'll be a small  production  um in the next two years or so  so i think we're very close this field  has made a huge leap  so by the end of the year we could see  it in supermarkets  on the shelves next to the vegetarian  options this real meat but that hasn't  been  an animal hasn't been killed in the  process no i i i refer to a prototype  so it's a small selective production  that you know  showcases this and allows the regulators  to approve it  very interesting and are there parts of  um meats that you can't replicate so  much  i'm thinking for example um things like  ribs like the bone part of things  is the meat that you create are they  going to be just lumps of meat  essentially so  all those other parts of an animal  presumably you can't replicate that  look what bioengineering um has  provided us uh within the last um  10 years is really tools to to really  think  think big uh and i do think that it's  possible to actually  create something that looks like a bone  3d printing of calcium etc  but let's be pragmatic the first product  that you want to bring into  the market is obviously something that's  very simple to produce  and the simplest products i would say  are minced meat  sausages and things like that but i  think  that already has the potential that a  real step  change in terms of how we think about  meat uh how we think about the origin of  meat  um and also it allows us to open up  a future where you know we are less  less reliant on on on  on the resources that really put a big  strain  on on our on our planet yes and let's  come to some of those pros in just a  second because people might be wondering  you know  why bother but first of all do you think  that this  tastes like meat um i mean i know that  biologically it is but are there parts  of an animal's growing experience all  around the world you hear about you know  grass-fed beef or whatever it is  part of the animal's diet or exercise  that makes the meat taste different  can you do anything about that when  you've only got the dna  well i do think if you that you actually  have even greater flexibility  um if you have something in an  industrial process  so it's much less  left to whatever happens to the outside  i think what i'm trying to say here is  once you apply  an engineering approach to biology and i  think this happens now broadly  uh across various spectra um  then then your your tool set your  abilities actually increase and you can  do  things that you may have not thought  about um  i can think of some really strange  things like  um creating creating  meat from different species and having  it in one  um so so fine tuning a bit like you know  a good wine  or or you could also you know oh  well like a like a meat with a dna of a  pig and a  and a cow and then seeing what that  would taste like that's right having  having those cells together in in in one  in one um you know piece of meat  essentially  hmm interesting i think we'll have to  get get viewers to email in on their  thoughts about that and what  combinations of me  sure i think we're talking about the  future at this point  yeah i think this technology has  has um a chance to address some of the  sort of  real pain points including  including the reason why you know we're  sat  stuck in zoom boxes at the moment and  not being able to travel freely i think  uh  you know intensive farming um  comes with the risk of uh viruses um  transmitting  from from from animals to human  yes absolutely and can you talk a little  bit about the benefits  that you see with with this approach is  one day we won't be have to kill animals  for farming  ever is that right yes absolutely i  think that's uh  one of the drivers animal welfare  considerations i think um on top of my  personal  uh agenda as well um although i do like  meat  i do eat meat so i don't want to sort of  pretend that i'm  a vegetarian at this point in time  the second aspect of course is um you  know the conditions under which animals  are you know raised uh  uh and and slaughtered i think here um  having an alternative is  is really really important  uh another aspect is um the  environmental impact  and also the medical impact of the  current way of producing meat  so we all heard about the use of  antibiotics  in farms and what that means is that  a lot of antibiotics are used across  across the globe which don't  help patients so to speak but make  bugs become more resistant  and we feel the effects of that  in in the clinic we have an increase in  ins in bugs and strains that cannot be  treated  with the regular set of antibiotics  so i think it's key one key aspect of  cultured meat is to be able to  sort of reduce the broad application of  antibiotics  as a doctor that's of course something  that that's really important  and and then there's this other aspect  that  that i think people are becoming more  more aware of  which is really the greenhouse gas um  that  that is emissions that are associated  especially with  uh cattle and  in fact i think it's one of the largest  single  contributors contributors to to  greenhouse gases  um and so having an alternative  in this space i think is also something  um that that is very valuable  and finally we all know that meat's not  always healthy  and and that's sometimes because of you  know  certain fats that are in in in meats  and if you let's go back to this  engineering approach what you could  what you could essentially do is you  know you could take out some of those  non-healthy um you know substances that  uh that we see although i think again  we're talking more about the future  yes of course um and is all of this  safe you know when we come talking about  um gm  foods for example a lot of people's  concerns are that is not safe and what  you're doing is not just mod it's not  modifying genetics obviously but you're  recreating it and you're growing  meat and which could be the main basis  of our diet  is that a safe thing to do to be  consuming i mean let's be honest  uh when we culture meat it's an  artificial process  is is a synthetic process um the  question then becomes  um is the is the product safe or not  and that's essentially so so you know i  think uh  um we just need to recognize that this  is not something that happens without  you know an industrial machine behind it  now the the the the good thing is that  um  we have a lot of experience  um you know our society was regulating  um aspects of food  and aspects of health you know there's a  system that checks every drug that is  developed there's a system that  uh looks at every new food uh that  that's being put out and and i and i do  trust that  um that the robustness of  of these systems um and so i do think uh  if you ever want to create a product  that can be used  it has to it has to jump all the hoops  and  and all the checks um that that are  required to make it a safe product  so i wouldn't feel comfortable at all um  if  we sort of decide if there would be  someone who to decide to go forward with  a product that isn't safe  yes absolutely well it's interesting  that you mentioned the regulators  because  in anthony brown's piece for the  spectator this week he talks about how  the singaporean regulators  have given the green light for something  like this but  in the eu for example there's a bit more  reluctance  and he mentions that in brussels and  washington lobbyists from  the farming industry have made have are  making efforts so that  the word meat can only be applied to  meat that comes from  animals being slaughtered i mean you  must be coming up against quite a lot of  opposition inertia  yeah i i do think if you come with  something  that's a very disruptive idea and  by no way in way i want to claim that  this was my idea i think there's a whole  field out there  but if you're part of something that's  novel  uh you you are going to ruffle feathers  and and and cause some pushback and i  think some of that pushback is  um is good because it um  basically means that you have to up your  game you have to show  uh that uh what what you generate what  you produce is actually better  in in in so many aspects um  i think there's also a little bit of  sort of fear  uh in certain pockets um um  what's interesting though is that um if  you look at  the sentiment of of of the users you  know the man in the street and the women  on the street  that we can see a real swing away from  you know  um from this is  artificial we don't want to touch it to  some  to to a sort of new mindset where people  are aware  of all the problems that our current  system has  and where people decide to become vegan  or vegetarian  not because they don't like meat but  because they don't like the process uh  and and and the animals the wealth  aspects  of it and and this particular sort of  um you know group of people which is  which is large um i believe um  is very in favor for for for a meat  alternative  even though uh it is it is you know  something that comes out of the lab  yes well as a family vegetarian i can  very much get behind that  and i'm intrigued by this idea of the  hybrid sausage  dr mark cotter thank you very much for  joining spectator tv  thank you so much and thanks to all our  guests on this episode and to you for  watching  as ever if you have any feedback please  email spectator tv  spectator dot co dot uk we do read all  of them even if we may not have time to  get back to you  and a final reminder if you enjoyed the  discussion today why not pick up the  magazine or read us online you'll get  more of these same intelligent  contributors  fresh topics and insightful analysis  without bumping into a pay wall  just go to spectator dot co dot e k  forward voucher to subscribe  12 issues for 12 pounds and we'll even  throw in a 20 pound amazon voucher  thanks for watching spectator tv and see  you next week  [Music]  you
 

